{"id":4259,"date":"2021-11-21T21:11:14","date_gmt":"2021-11-22T05:11:14","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4259"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:35:41","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:35:41","slug":"lesson-1-leaf-cycle","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/lesson-1-leaf-cycle\/","title":{"rendered":"Lesson 1: Leaf Cycle"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bb82f8b\" name=\"6a2214bb82f8b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Why do leaves change color?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">At the beginning of spring, leaves start as tiny buds and grow through the summer, where they reach their full size. When summer ends, the autumn leaf cycle begins! This is when spring and summer rainfall decreases, and trees in mountain areas get less water from snow runoff. The days are getting shorter, which means less sunlight and colder temperatures. This is a problem for trees since their food source is sunlight and water (you might know this process as <\/span><b>photosynthesis<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">). So trees cut off nutrients like <\/span><b>chlorophyll <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">to the leaves by forming a layer of special cork cells at the base of each leaf. This keeps the tree\u2019s trunk, branches, and roots alive during the winter while living off stored food until spring. Chlorophyll is what makes plants green, so as it disappears, tree leaves\u2019 true colors, like yellow and orange, start to come out.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">As chlorophyll production stops, <\/span><b>anthocyanin<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> production starts. Anthocyanin is a pigment that appears red in trees but can also be red, purple, blue, and black elsewhere in nature. Some trees have more anthocyanin than others, so the yellow and orange stage is skipped and the leaves turn red right away. However, this process isn\u2019t always uniform. One tree, and one leaf, can have multiple colors!\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Brown leaves are a result of two things: sunlight and cold. If the fall season is very sunny, the sunlight will break down the pigments in the leaves, resulting in brown leaves. This doesn\u2019t necessarily mean the leaves are dead! The only thing that can really kill a leaf is the cold.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">At the end of the autumn leaf cycle, the leaves fall to the ground and decomposers turn the leaves into food for the soil.<\/span><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bb830e2\" name=\"6a2214bb830e2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Flight Check!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Before diving into coding the leaf cycle, make sure you have done the setup lessons in the &#8220;Getting Started&#8221; module. These lessons include:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/0-2-software-installation-updated\/\">Install Python<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/0-3-pycharm-edu-orientation-updated\/\">Install and setup Pycharm Edu<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/0-4-flying-with-remote-control-updated\/\">Learn how to use the remote control<\/a><\/li>\n<li><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/0-5-setting-the-trim-updated\/\">Know how to trim your drone<\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Now you are all set! Move on to the next step for an introduction to basic flight commands.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bb8316e\" name=\"6a2214bb8316e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Flight Events Practice<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Create a new file called &#8220;flightevents.py&#8221;. Review the lesson on using PyCharm if you need to. Then, copy this line of code.<\/p>\n<p>Connect your drone remote to your computer using a micro USB cable, then connect the battery into the drone. Place your drone somewhere safe on the ground. When you&#8217;re ready, press the green triangle. What happens?<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.hover(3)\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Your drone should have taken off, hovered for 3 seconds, then landed. To program CoDrone Mini in Python, you will always need the <strong>first three lines<\/strong> of code so keep them handy.<\/p>\n<p>Try changing the number, also called a <strong>parameter<\/strong>, inside of hover(), which is a <strong>function<\/strong>. All functions will always have parentheses, but not all functions take parameters. What happens when you change the number?<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.hover(5) \r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>You can also take off and land many times in a row!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini \r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone() \r\ndrone.pair() \r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff() \r\ndrone.land() \r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff() \r\ndrone.land() \r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff() \r\ndrone.land() \r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bb831f9\" name=\"6a2214bb831f9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Flight Movements<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]There are four basic flight movements: roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle. Each direction can be positive, zero, or negative.[\/vc_column_text][vc_images_carousel images=&#8221;642,643,644,645&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221;][vc_column_text]For example, here is how you would take off and move forward (positive pitch) for 2 seconds.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.set_pitch(50)\r\ndrone.move(2)\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>A few things to notice:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The top 3 lines are at the beginning of the code. This pairs and sets up the drone.<\/li>\n<li>The function to set the forward speed is set_pitch(). To go forward, put a positive number from 1-100.<\/li>\n<li>The set function must be accompanied by the move() function. The duration of the movement in seconds is the <strong>parameter.<\/strong><\/li>\n<li>Finally, don&#8217;t forget to land and close!<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>You can also fly backward.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.set_pitch(-20)\r\ndrone.move(3)\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>You can also combine movements! Here is how you fly in a diagonal to the back and to the right.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.set_pitch(-30)\r\ndrone.set_roll(30)\r\ndrone.move(2)\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Be careful when you use multiple directions in a flight program. If you set a value, the drone will keep that value for the whole program unless you change it back to zero! For example use drone.set_pitch(0) if you want the drone to stop flying forward with the next movement.<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>Experiment with all four flight movements, positive and negative. When you&#8217;re ready for your challenge, see the next step.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bb83278\" name=\"6a2214bb83278\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Leaf Cycle Introduction<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You will be coding your drone to &#8220;fall&#8221; like a leaf. Your drone will rise up in the air, then sway side to side while falling down and changing the LED color.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;4295&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]It helps to write pseudocode before going to write your program. Pseudocode is when you write your code in &#8220;plain language&#8221;, or in a way that someone who may not be a programmer can understand. It is almost like a plan for what your code should look like in the end.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Takeoff and throttle up as high as they can without getting too close to the ceiling.\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Turn the LED green and \u201cfall\u201d while rolling to the right<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Turn the LED yellow and \u201cfall\u201d while rolling to the left<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Turn the LED orange and \u201cfall\u201d while rolling to the right<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Turn the LED red and \u201cfall\u201d while rolling to the left<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Land\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>For now, do not worry about the LEDs, just program the flight movements. To &#8220;fall&#8221;, you can set the throttle to -15 while switching your roll parameter from right to left.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bb832e0\" name=\"6a2214bb832e0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Leaf Cycle Code : Part 1<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Your code should look something like this. You can change the values of the parameters depending on how much room you have for your drone to fly in.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n# Take off and fly up to the ceiling\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.set_throttle(50)\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\n#First fall\r\ndrone.set_throttle(-15)\r\ndrone.set_roll(40)\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\n#second fall\r\ndrone.set_roll(-40)\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\n#third fall\r\ndrone.set_roll(40)\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\n#fourth fall\r\ndrone.set_roll(-40)\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\n#land\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Note:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>It can be helpful to separate your code with spaces in between the different sections<\/li>\n<li>The lines starting with a pound # symbol are <strong>comments<\/strong>. These are notes for you that the computer will ignore.<\/li>\n<li>Since the set_throttle(-15) applies for all of the falling flight movements, you only need to set it once.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bb8334f\" name=\"6a2214bb8334f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lights<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Your drone now flies like a leaf, but it doesn&#8217;t look like one yet. The last step is to add code to change the drone&#8217;s LED, which\u00a0stands for \u201clight-emitting diode\u201d. Your CoDrone Mini has 1 RGB LED on the main body. RGB stands for red, green, and blue. Using the three colors, you can create almost every color imaginable!<\/p>\n<p>The most basic LED function that you can use is LED_color. This function allows you to change the color of the LED built into the drone. Watch the video below to learn more about this function and its parameters:[\/vc_column_text][vc_video][vc_column_text]From the video and a bit of Googling, you know that you can make the four colors of the leaf cycle using this code and RGB values.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.LED_color(0, 255, 0, 100) # Green\r\n\r\ndrone.LED_color(255, 255, 0, 100) # Yellow\r\n\r\ndrone.LED_color(255, 165, 0, 100) # Orange\r\n\r\ndrone.LED_color(255, 0, 0, 100)   # Red<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bb833a3\" name=\"6a2214bb833a3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Leaf Cycle Code : Part 2<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now that you know a little bit about how to change the drone&#8217;s LED, add the code into the leaf cycle program. Your end result will look something like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.set_throttle(50)\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_throttle(-10)\r\n\r\ndrone.LED_color(0,255,0,100) # green\r\ndrone.set_roll(40)\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.LED_color(255,255,0,100)# yellow\r\ndrone.set_roll(-40)\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.LED_color(255,165,0,100)# orange\r\ndrone.set_roll(40)\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.LED_color(255,0,0,100) # red\r\ndrone.set_roll(-40)\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214bb833fd\" name=\"6a2214bb833fd\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Extra Activity: F-A-L-L<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now that you have a general understanding of flight movements, it&#8217;s time for a challenge.<\/p>\n<p>Pick a letter in the word &#8220;fall&#8221; and code your drone to fly in the shape of that letter. Make multiple programs for each letter for an extra challenge and run them in order to spell out the whole word.<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>The letter &#8220;L&#8221; might look like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.set_pitch(-50)\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(0) # reset pitch to zero so it stops moving backward\r\ndrone.set_roll(50) \r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 10 Why do leaves change color? [vc_column_text]At the beginning of spring, leaves start as tiny buds and grow through the summer, where they reach their full size. When&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/lesson-1-leaf-cycle\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4316,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3473,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2125,3726,2122,2134,3727],"class_list":["post-4259","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-fall","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-seasonal","module-seasonal","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4259","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":6,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4259\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":4318,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4259\/revisions\/4318"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4316"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4259"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4259"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4259"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}